<Layout title="AI Customer Chatbot Platform" description="A frontend perspective on understandable conversational interfaces, explicit interaction states, accessibility, and reusable UI foundations.">
  <header class="detail-hero wrap"><a class="back" href="/#work">← Selected work</a><div class="eyebrow">02 / AI interfaces</div><h1>AI Customer<br />Chatbot Platform.<br /><span class="serif">Clarity at every step.</span></h1><p class="detail-deck">A frontend perspective on making a conversational product understandable, accessible, and resilient when the answer is not immediate.</p><div class="detail-meta"><div><small>Focus</small><p>Customer-facing conversational UI</p></div><div><small>Engineering lens</small><p>Component boundaries & interaction states</p></div><div><small>Presentation</small><p>Sanitized project overview & design considerations</p></div></div></header>
  <div class="wrap case-layout"><aside class="toc" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#context">The challenge</a><a href="#boundaries">Platform boundaries</a><a href="#states">Interaction states</a><a href="#accessibility">Accessibility & trust</a><a href="#ownership">Engineering ownership</a></aside><div class="case-body">
  <section id="context"><h2>The interface carries the uncertainty.</h2><p>In a conversational experience, a customer needs to understand more than the response. They need to know whether their message was sent, whether the system is still working, and what they can do when something goes wrong.</p><p>This case study frames the AI Customer Chatbot Platform through frontend engineering. Detailed implementation history and measured outcomes have not been supplied, so the architecture and behaviors below are presented as design considerations, rather than claims about shipped features.</p></section>
  <section id="boundaries"><h2>Keep the conversation UI understandable.</h2><figure class="diagram"><div class="diagram-label">Illustrative boundaries / proposed reference model</div><div class="diagram-flow"><div class="diagram-node"><strong>Conversation UI</strong><small>Messages · Composer · Feedback</small></div><span class="diagram-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="diagram-node"><strong>Interaction state</strong><small>Pending · Responding · Error</small></div><span class="diagram-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="diagram-node"><strong>Service boundary</strong><small>Requests · Responses · Cancellation</small></div></div><figcaption>This intentionally omits model providers, internal services, proprietary prompts, and customer information.</figcaption></figure><p>A useful separation keeps visual components focused on presentation, interaction state focused on the conversation lifecycle, and service integration behind a clear boundary. This gives teams a way to reason about failures without making every UI component understand the backend.</p><h3>Reuse the foundation; respect the interaction</h3><p>A design system can provide the typography, spacing, controls, and accessible patterns. Conversation-specific behavior still deserves its own design: message order, response progress, recovery actions, and the relationship between the transcript and composer.</p></section>
  <section id="states"><h2>Design the moments between messages.</h2><p>The happy path is only one part of the experience. A clear interaction model should cover the transitions a customer can actually encounter.</p><ul><li><strong>Ready:</strong> make the input’s purpose and available actions clear.</li><li><strong>Pending:</strong> acknowledge submission and prevent accidental duplicate requests.</li><li><strong>Responding:</strong> communicate progress without disrupting reading or keyboard focus.</li><li><strong>Interrupted or failed:</strong> explain the state in plain language and offer an explicit recovery path.</li><li><strong>Complete:</strong> make the next action available while preserving the conversation context.</li></ul><p>Streaming can improve perceived responsiveness, but it introduces partial content and cancellation states. A single complete response is simpler, but may require stronger progress feedback. The right choice depends on service behavior and the customer’s task.</p></section>
  <section id="accessibility"><h2>Trust is built through small details.</h2><h3>Let people stay in control</h3><p>New messages should not unexpectedly move focus. Screen-reader announcements need to be useful without reading every token as it arrives. Long conversations need a deliberate approach to scroll position and new-message awareness.</p><h3>Make uncertainty visible</h3><p>Progress, errors, and unavailable actions should be communicated with text as well as visual styling. Interface language should help customers understand what the system is doing without overstating what an AI response can guarantee.</p><h3>Keep examples safe to share</h3><p>The portfolio illustration uses fictional content. No customer messages, proprietary prompts, service endpoints, or internal product screenshots are reproduced here.</p></section>
  <section id="ownership"><h2>Bring platform thinking to AI UI.</h2><p>The engineering approach I bring is grounded in the same disciplines as design-system work: explicit component APIs, TypeScript contracts, accessible interaction patterns, documentation, and PR reviews that consider the complete user experience.</p><p>A strong technical discussion starts with the decisions and their evidence: which states the service supports, how failure recovery behaves, how keyboard and screen-reader users experience updates, and how teams can extend the interface without fragmenting its behavior.</p><div class="callout">This overview is deliberately qualitative. A future revision can add verified responsibilities, implementation decisions, and outcomes once those details are available for public use.</div></section><a class="next-case" href="/work/elements/"><span>Explore / Elements Enterprise Design System</span><span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
  </div></div>
